{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-162563" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068126/assets/638a02dec15c2cecd8563854c2fb071ff70f10004f60339ef92d0914e7198ebc.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨4{x}^{3}+3{y}^{2}+2z,\\,0,\\,0⟩$ and the surface $S$ defined by the bounding region of the rectangular solid shown above in the first octant. Calculate the flux of $F$ through $S.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068126/state.json" "file_sha256" "1a50a43e95841a894c960cbfa2978f95d81e693b1c46f1daf30209d69ffe587e"} {"question" "q-162563" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $n,$ and $F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nNotice that only the $x$-component of $F=⟨4{x}^{3}+3{y}^{2}+2z,\\,0,\\,0⟩$ is non-zero. Since the lateral sides of $S$ are parallel to the $x$-axis, the flux of $F$ through $S$ will be non-zero only at the front and back sides. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068126/assets/4fd228a9bf0a2d8382139d3f804ee7c9b3fd8721c8ad079de43e61b9e8947240.png)\n\n  \n\nLet's consider the flux of $F$ through these two sides.\n\n- Let ${S}_{1}$ denote the front surface. On this surface, we have $x=1$ for $0\\le y\\le 1$ and $0\\le z\\le 3.$ The outward-pointing unit normal vector to ${S}_{1}$ is\n\n$$\n{n}_{1}=⟨1,\\,0,\\,0⟩.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $x=1$ on ${S}_{1},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot {n}_{1} & =⟨4{x}^{3}+3{y}^{2}+2z,\\,0,\\,0⟩\\cdot ⟨1,\\,0,\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨4+3{y}^{2}+2z,\\,0,\\,0⟩\\cdot ⟨1,\\,0,\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =4+3{y}^{2}+2z.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the flux of $F$ across ${S}_{1}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}4+3{y}^{2}+2z\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}4+3{y}^{2}+2z\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}4+3{y}^{2}+2z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{[(4+2z)y+{y}^{3}]}_{y=0}^{y=1}\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}4+2z+1\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}2z+5\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & ={[{z}^{2}+5z]}_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =9+15 \\\\  & =24\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Let ${S}_{2}$ denote the back surface. On this surface, we have $x=0$ for $0\\le y\\le 1$ and $0\\le z\\le 3.$ The outward-pointing unit normal vector to ${S}_{2}$ is\n\n$$\n{n}_{2}=⟨-1,\\,0,\\,0⟩.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $x=0$ on ${S}_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot {n}_{2} & =⟨4{x}^{3}+3{y}^{2}+2z,\\,0,\\,0⟩\\cdot ⟨-1,\\,0,\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3{y}^{2}+2z,\\,0,\\,0⟩\\cdot ⟨-1,\\,0,\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =-(3{y}^{2}+2z).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the flux of $F$ across ${S}_{2}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{2}}-(3{y}^{2}+2z)\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =-{∬}_{D}3{y}^{2}+2z\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}3{y}^{2}+2z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{0}^{3}{[{y}^{3}+2yz]}_{y=0}^{y=1}\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{0}^{3}1+2z\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =-{[z+{z}^{2}]}_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =-(3+9) \\\\  & =-12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S+{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =24+(-12) \\\\  & =12.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068126/state.json" "file_sha256" "1a50a43e95841a894c960cbfa2978f95d81e693b1c46f1daf30209d69ffe587e"} {"question" "q-162563" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068126/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "d6d75d30e7c8717e7da46673aa7f12d35bcdbdfe33e4bb63e23f33b6072f04ce"} {"question" "q-162563" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068126/state.json" "file_sha256" "1a50a43e95841a894c960cbfa2978f95d81e693b1c46f1daf30209d69ffe587e"} {"question" "q-162563" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "12"] ["math" "15"] ["math" "6"] ["math" "8"] ["math" "9"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068126/state.json" "file_sha256" "1a50a43e95841a894c960cbfa2978f95d81e693b1c46f1daf30209d69ffe587e"} {"question" "q-162563" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "12"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068126/state.json" "file_sha256" "1a50a43e95841a894c960cbfa2978f95d81e693b1c46f1daf30209d69ffe587e"} {"question" "q-161222" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068126/assets/01902836fd1e91ac829f67a279103a9b3d5732d7b058f03c695b6733a42e673d.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨0,\\,0,\\,{x}^{3}+{y}^{2}+z⟩$ and the surface $S$ defined by the bounding region of the rectangular solid shown above in the first octant. Calculate the flux of $F$ through $S.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068126/state.json" "file_sha256" "1a50a43e95841a894c960cbfa2978f95d81e693b1c46f1daf30209d69ffe587e"} {"question" "q-161222" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $n,$ and $F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nNotice that only the $z$-component of $F=⟨0,\\,0,\\,{x}^{3}+{y}^{2}+z⟩$ is non-zero. Since the lateral sides of $S$ are parallel to the $z$-axis, the flux of $F$ through $S$ will be non-zero only at the top and bottom sides. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068126/assets/c058465d6ca7855446711ebc44f6b23913ee786d2fd9bb32ae6c671964e1ad32.png)\n\n \n\nLet's consider the flux of $F$ through these two sides.\n\n- Let ${S}_{1}$ denote the top surface. On this surface, we have $z=1$ for $0\\le x\\le 1$ and $0\\le y\\le 1.$ The outward-pointing unit normal vector to ${S}_{1}$ is\n\n$$\n{n}_{1}=⟨0,\\,0,\\,1⟩.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $z=1$ on ${S}_{1},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot {n}_{1} & =⟨0,\\,0,\\,{x}^{3}+{y}^{2}+z⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,0,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,0,\\,{x}^{3}+{y}^{2}+1⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,0,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & ={x}^{3}+{y}^{2}+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the flux of $F$ across ${S}_{1}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}{x}^{3}+{y}^{2}+1\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}{x}^{3}+{y}^{2}+1\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{3}+{y}^{2}+1\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{[\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{4}+x{y}^{2}+x]}_{x=0}^{x=1}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{y}^{2}+\\frac{5}{4}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={[\\frac{1}{3}{y}^{3}+\\frac{5}{4}y]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{19}{12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Let ${S}_{2}$ denote the bottom surface. On this surface, we have $z=0$ for $0\\le x\\le 1$ and $0\\le y\\le 1.$ The outward-pointing unit normal vector to ${S}_{2}$ is\n\n$$\n{n}_{2}=⟨0,\\,0,\\,-1⟩.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $z=0$ on ${S}_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot {n}_{2} & =⟨0,\\,0,\\,{x}^{3}+{y}^{2}+z⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,0,\\,-1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,0,\\,{x}^{3}+{y}^{2}⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,0,\\,-1⟩ \\\\  & =-({x}^{3}+{y}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the flux of $F$ across ${S}_{2}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{2}}-({x}^{3}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =-{∬}_{D}{x}^{3}+{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{3}+{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{0}^{1}{[\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{4}+x{y}^{2}]}_{x=0}^{x=1}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{0}^{1}{y}^{2}+\\frac{1}{4}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =-{[\\frac{1}{3}{y}^{3}+\\frac{1}{4}y]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{7}{12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S+{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =\\frac{19}{12}+(-\\frac{7}{12}) \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068126/state.json" "file_sha256" "1a50a43e95841a894c960cbfa2978f95d81e693b1c46f1daf30209d69ffe587e"} {"question" "q-161222" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068126/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "d6d75d30e7c8717e7da46673aa7f12d35bcdbdfe33e4bb63e23f33b6072f04ce"} {"question" "q-161222" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068126/state.json" "file_sha256" "1a50a43e95841a894c960cbfa2978f95d81e693b1c46f1daf30209d69ffe587e"} {"question" "q-161222" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "1"] ["math" "3"] ["math" "\\frac{11}{12}"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{6}"] ["math" "\\frac{5}{12}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068126/state.json" "file_sha256" "1a50a43e95841a894c960cbfa2978f95d81e693b1c46f1daf30209d69ffe587e"} {"question" "q-161222" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "1"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068126/state.json" "file_sha256" "1a50a43e95841a894c960cbfa2978f95d81e693b1c46f1daf30209d69ffe587e"} {"question" "q-162560" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068126/assets/04f58e972500033523e3ca3247ea4e1c5c377d8dc2b402b1c0200d9c85529894.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨0,\\,3{x}^{2}y+2z,\\,0⟩$ and the surface $S$ defined by the bounding region of the rectangular solid shown above in the first octant. Calculate the flux of $F$ through $S.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068126/state.json" "file_sha256" "1a50a43e95841a894c960cbfa2978f95d81e693b1c46f1daf30209d69ffe587e"} {"question" "q-162560" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $n,$ and $F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nNotice that only the $y$-component of $F=⟨0,\\,3{x}^{2}y+2z,\\,0⟩$ is non-zero. Since the lateral sides of $S$ are parallel to the $y$-axis, the flux of $F$ through $S$ will be non-zero only at the right and left sides. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068126/assets/8652a197f603c517cd462c69473e3a693ba8dcd76340b6241f890663356e00cb.png)\n\n \n\nLet's consider the flux of $F$ through these two sides.\n\n- Let ${S}_{1}$ denote the right surface. On this surface, we have $y=2$ for $0\\le x\\le 2$ and $0\\le z\\le 1.$ The outward-pointing unit normal vector to ${S}_{1}$ is\n\n$$\n{n}_{1}=⟨0,\\,1,\\,0⟩.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $y=2$ on ${S}_{1},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot {n}_{1} & =⟨0,\\,3{x}^{2}y+2z,\\,0⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,1,\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,6{x}^{2}+2z,\\,0⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,1,\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =6{x}^{2}+2z.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the flux of $F$ across ${S}_{1}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}6{x}^{2}+2z\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}6{x}^{2}+2z\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}6{x}^{2}+2z\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{[2{x}^{3}+2xz]}_{x=0}^{x=2}\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}16+4z\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & ={[16z+2{z}^{2}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =18\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Let ${S}_{2}$ denote the left surface. On this surface, we have $y=0$ for $0\\le x\\le 2$ and $0\\le z\\le 1.$ The outward-pointing unit normal vector to ${S}_{2}$ is\n\n$$\n{n}_{2}=⟨0,\\,-1,\\,0⟩.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $y=0$ on ${S}_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot {n}_{2} & =⟨0,\\,3{x}^{2}y+2z,\\,0⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,-1,\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,2z,\\,0⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,-1,\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =-2z.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the flux of $F$ across ${S}_{2}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{2}}-2z\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =-2{∬}_{D}z\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-2{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}z\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =-2{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x{\\int }_{0}^{1}z\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =-2\\cdot [x]_{0}^{2}\\cdot {[\\frac{{z}^{2}}{2}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =-2\\cdot 2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S+{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =18+(-2) \\\\  & =16.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068126/state.json" "file_sha256" "1a50a43e95841a894c960cbfa2978f95d81e693b1c46f1daf30209d69ffe587e"} {"question" "q-162560" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068126/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "d6d75d30e7c8717e7da46673aa7f12d35bcdbdfe33e4bb63e23f33b6072f04ce"} {"question" "q-162560" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068126/state.json" "file_sha256" "1a50a43e95841a894c960cbfa2978f95d81e693b1c46f1daf30209d69ffe587e"} {"question" "q-162560" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "12"] ["math" "16"] ["math" "18"] ["math" "20"] ["math" "24"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068126/state.json" "file_sha256" "1a50a43e95841a894c960cbfa2978f95d81e693b1c46f1daf30209d69ffe587e"} {"question" "q-162560" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "16"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068126/state.json" "file_sha256" "1a50a43e95841a894c960cbfa2978f95d81e693b1c46f1daf30209d69ffe587e"} {"question" "q-161162" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068126/assets/8901e7155a476f0019b6d66e3b62b579b91daf99a448e497c93efce1d94415b0.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨0,\\,0,\\,2x+yz⟩$ and the surface $S$ defined by the bounding region of the rectangular solid shown above in the first octant. Calculate the flux of $F$ through $S.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068126/state.json" "file_sha256" "1a50a43e95841a894c960cbfa2978f95d81e693b1c46f1daf30209d69ffe587e"} {"question" "q-161162" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $n,$ and $F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nNotice that only the $z$-component of $F=⟨0,\\,0,\\,2x+yz⟩$ is non-zero. Since the lateral sides of $S$ are parallel to the $z$-axis, the flux of $F$ through $S$ will be non-zero only at the top and bottom sides. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068126/assets/8fa71e16d84eb92506e9b10269182f907cb4f2437d0a9bdd3822da48c4cbba4a.png)\n\n \n\nLet's consider the flux of $F$ through these two sides.\n\n- Let ${S}_{1}$ denote the top surface. On this surface, we have $z=2$ for $0\\le x\\le 1$ and $0\\le y\\le 1.$ The outward-pointing unit normal vector to ${S}_{1}$ is\n\n$$\n{n}_{1}=⟨0,\\,0,\\,1⟩.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $z=2$ on ${S}_{1},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot {n}_{1} & =⟨0,\\,0,\\,2x+yz⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,0,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,0,\\,2x+2y⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,0,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =2x+2y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the flux of $F$ across ${S}_{1}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}{S}_{1} & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}2x+2y\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2x+2y\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}2x+2y\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{[{x}^{2}+2xy]}_{x=0}^{x=1}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}1+2y\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={[y+{y}^{2}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Let ${S}_{2}$ denote the bottom surface. On this surface, we have $z=0$ for $0\\le x\\le 1$ and $0\\le y\\le 1.$ The outward-pointing unit normal vector to ${S}_{2}$ is\n\n$$\n{n}_{2}=⟨0,\\,0,\\,-1⟩.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $z=0$ on ${S}_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot {n}_{2} & =⟨0,\\,0,\\,2x+yz⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,0,\\,-1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,0,\\,2x⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,0,\\,-1⟩ \\\\  & =-2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the flux of $F$ across ${S}_{2}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}{S}_{2} & ={∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{2}}-2x\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}-2x\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}-2x\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\,\\text{d}y{\\int }_{0}^{1}2x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-1\\cdot {[{x}^{2}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =-1\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S+{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =2+(-1) \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068126/state.json" "file_sha256" "1a50a43e95841a894c960cbfa2978f95d81e693b1c46f1daf30209d69ffe587e"} {"question" "q-161162" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068126/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "d6d75d30e7c8717e7da46673aa7f12d35bcdbdfe33e4bb63e23f33b6072f04ce"} {"question" "q-161162" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068126/state.json" "file_sha256" "1a50a43e95841a894c960cbfa2978f95d81e693b1c46f1daf30209d69ffe587e"} {"question" "q-161162" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "0"] ["math" "1"] ["math" "2"] ["math" "3"] ["math" "4"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068126/state.json" "file_sha256" "1a50a43e95841a894c960cbfa2978f95d81e693b1c46f1daf30209d69ffe587e"} {"question" "q-161162" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "1"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068126/state.json" "file_sha256" "1a50a43e95841a894c960cbfa2978f95d81e693b1c46f1daf30209d69ffe587e"} {"question" "q-161219" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068126/assets/9afc7f79f4612ce579e82a6b3c5086922d14051978907bfeb5c133a3f99791c7.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨0,\\,0,\\,x{y}^{2}+z⟩$ and the surface $S$ defined by the bounding region of the rectangular solid shown above in the first octant. Calculate the flux of $F$ through $S.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068126/state.json" "file_sha256" "1a50a43e95841a894c960cbfa2978f95d81e693b1c46f1daf30209d69ffe587e"} {"question" "q-161219" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $n,$ and $F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nNotice that only the $z$-component of $F=⟨0,\\,0,\\,x{y}^{2}+z⟩$ is non-zero. Since the lateral sides of $S$ are parallel to the $z$-axis, the flux of $F$ through $S$ will be non-zero only at the top and bottom sides. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068126/assets/fa4986b1de44fa4ccc75114e64abfd93d0de16618f2f1b29695d9115019b6e3e.png)\n\n \n\nLet's consider the flux of $F$ through these two sides.\n\n- Let ${S}_{1}$ denote the top surface. On this surface, we have $z=4$ for $0\\le x\\le 2$ and $0\\le y\\le 1.$ The outward-pointing unit normal vector to ${S}_{1}$ is\n\n$$\n{n}_{1}=⟨0,\\,0,\\,1⟩.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $z=4$ on ${S}_{1},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot {n}_{1} & =⟨0,\\,0,\\,x{y}^{2}+z⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,0,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,0,\\,x{y}^{2}+4⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,0,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =x{y}^{2}+4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the flux of $F$ across ${S}_{1}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}x{y}^{2}+4\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}x{y}^{2}+4\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}x{y}^{2}+4\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{[\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+4x]}_{x=0}^{x=2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}2{y}^{2}+8\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={[\\frac{2}{3}{y}^{3}+8y]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{26}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Let ${S}_{2}$ denote the bottom surface. On this surface, we have $z=0$ for $0\\le x\\le 2$ and $0\\le y\\le 1.$ The outward-pointing unit normal vector to ${S}_{2}$ is\n\n$$\n{n}_{2}=⟨0,\\,0,\\,-1⟩.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $z=0$ on ${S}_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot {n}_{2} & =⟨0,\\,0,\\,x{y}^{2}+z⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,0,\\,-1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,0,\\,x{y}^{2}⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,0,\\,-1⟩ \\\\  & =-x{y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the flux of $F$ across ${S}_{2}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{2}}-x{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =-{∬}_{D}x{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}x{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{0}^{1}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y{\\int }_{0}^{2}x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{[\\frac{1}{3}{y}^{3}]}_{0}^{1}\\cdot {[\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S+{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =\\frac{26}{3}+(-\\frac{2}{3}) \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068126/state.json" "file_sha256" "1a50a43e95841a894c960cbfa2978f95d81e693b1c46f1daf30209d69ffe587e"} {"question" "q-161219" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068126/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "d6d75d30e7c8717e7da46673aa7f12d35bcdbdfe33e4bb63e23f33b6072f04ce"} {"question" "q-161219" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068126/state.json" "file_sha256" "1a50a43e95841a894c960cbfa2978f95d81e693b1c46f1daf30209d69ffe587e"} {"question" "q-161219" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "10"] ["math" "6"] ["math" "8"] ["math" "\\frac{19}{3}"] ["math" "\\frac{28}{3}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068126/state.json" "file_sha256" "1a50a43e95841a894c960cbfa2978f95d81e693b1c46f1daf30209d69ffe587e"} {"question" "q-161219" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "8"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068126/state.json" "file_sha256" "1a50a43e95841a894c960cbfa2978f95d81e693b1c46f1daf30209d69ffe587e"} {"question" "q-161203" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068126/assets/cece4e226becb315d6d951a8ae782750da79209d9a67ec52a8cd4d134999e9e6.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨0,\\,0,\\,z(4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2})⟩$ and the surface $S$ defined by the bounding region of a right circular cylinder of height $4$ whose base is a circle of radius $3$ centered at the origin, as shown above. Calculate the flux of $F$ through $S.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068126/state.json" "file_sha256" "1a50a43e95841a894c960cbfa2978f95d81e693b1c46f1daf30209d69ffe587e"} {"question" "q-161203" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $n,$ and $F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nNotice that only the $z$-component of $F=⟨0,\\,0,\\,z(4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2})⟩$ is non-zero. Since the lateral side of $S$ is parallel to the $z$-axis, the flux of $F$ through $S$ will be non-zero only at the top and bottom sides. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068126/assets/21e29d99d2d5330db2d946d981e4bd0f820ce26297bf95f962cb3000f0998136.png)\n\n \nLet's consider the flux of $F$ through these two sides.\n\n- Let ${S}_{1}$ denote the top surface. On this surface, we have $z=4$ for ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 9.$ The outward-pointing unit normal vector to ${S}_{1}$ is\n\n$$\n{n}_{1}=⟨0,\\,0,\\,1⟩.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $z=4$ on ${S}_{1},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot {n}_{1} & =⟨0,\\,0,\\,z(4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2})⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,0,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,0,\\,4(4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2})⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,0,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =4(4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the flux of $F$ across ${S}_{1}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}4(4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =4{∬}_{D}4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying a change of variables $(x,y)\\to (r,\\theta ),\\text{d}A=r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{∬}_{D}4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A & =4{∬}_{D}(4-{r}^{2})\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{3}4r-{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}\\theta {\\int }_{0}^{3}4r-{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r \\\\  & =4\\cdot 2\\pi \\cdot {[2{r}^{2}-\\frac{1}{4}{r}^{4}]}_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =8\\pi \\cdot (18-\\frac{81}{4}) \\\\  & =-18\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Let ${S}_{2}$ denote the bottom surface. On this surface, we have $z=0$ for ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 9.$ Therefore, for everywhere on ${S}_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\nF(x,y,0)=⟨0,\\,0,\\,0(4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2})⟩=⟨0,\\,0,\\,0⟩.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{2}}0\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{2}}0\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S+{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =-18\\pi +0 \\\\  & =-18\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068126/state.json" "file_sha256" "1a50a43e95841a894c960cbfa2978f95d81e693b1c46f1daf30209d69ffe587e"} {"question" "q-161203" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068126/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "d6d75d30e7c8717e7da46673aa7f12d35bcdbdfe33e4bb63e23f33b6072f04ce"} {"question" "q-161203" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068126/state.json" "file_sha256" "1a50a43e95841a894c960cbfa2978f95d81e693b1c46f1daf30209d69ffe587e"} {"question" "q-161203" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-18\\pi "] ["math" "-\\frac{11\\pi }{4}"] ["math" "-\\frac{5\\pi }{3}"] ["math" "12\\pi "] ["math" "\\frac{17\\pi }{3}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068126/state.json" "file_sha256" "1a50a43e95841a894c960cbfa2978f95d81e693b1c46f1daf30209d69ffe587e"} {"question" "q-161203" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-18\\pi "] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068126/state.json" "file_sha256" "1a50a43e95841a894c960cbfa2978f95d81e693b1c46f1daf30209d69ffe587e"} {"question" "q-161207" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068126/assets/4cb2c8128f24adddacd82b5f477848faa47ae52f95a5bb0636a8b286a6628a08.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨0,\\,0,\\,{z}^{2}\\sqrt{16-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}⟩$ and the surface $S$ defined by the bounding region of a right circular cylinder of height $2$ whose base is a circle of radius $2$ centered at the origin, as shown above. Calculate the flux of $F$ through $S.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068126/state.json" "file_sha256" "1a50a43e95841a894c960cbfa2978f95d81e693b1c46f1daf30209d69ffe587e"} {"question" "q-161207" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $n,$ and $F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nNotice that only the $z$-component of $F=⟨0,\\,0,\\,{z}^{2}\\sqrt{16-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}⟩$ is non-zero. Since the lateral side of $S$ is parallel to the $z$-axis, the flux of $F$ through $S$ will be non-zero only at the top and bottom sides. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068126/assets/e6fe01196298c1da0961fd01c73ee4cb7c71b6a03a027cceaba0e68be944c968.png)\n\n \nLet's consider the flux of $F$ through these two sides.\n\n- Let ${S}_{1}$ denote the top surface. On this surface, we have $z=2$ for ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 4.$ The outward-pointing unit normal vector to ${S}_{1}$ is\n\n$$\n{n}_{1}=⟨0,\\,0,\\,1⟩.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $z=2$ on ${S}_{1},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot {n}_{1} & =⟨0,\\,0,\\,{z}^{2}\\sqrt{16-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,0,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,0,\\,{2}^{2}\\sqrt{16-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,0,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{16-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the flux of $F$ across ${S}_{1}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}4\\sqrt{16-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =4{∬}_{D}\\sqrt{16-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying a change of variables $(x,y)\\to (r,\\theta ),\\text{d}A=r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{∬}_{D}\\sqrt{16-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A & =4{∬}_{D}\\sqrt{16-{r}^{2}}\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2}r\\sqrt{16-{r}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}\\theta {\\int }_{0}^{2}r\\sqrt{16-{r}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}r \\\\  & =4\\cdot 2\\pi \\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{2}r\\sqrt{16-{r}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}r \\\\  & =8\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}r\\sqrt{16-{r}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}r.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting $u=16-{r}^{2},$ we get $\\text{d}u=-2r\\,\\text{d}r.$ Also, the limits must be changed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & 0 & 2 \\\\ u & 16 & 12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSubstituting these into our integral, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}r\\sqrt{16-{r}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}r & =8\\pi \\cdot {\\int }_{16}^{12}\\sqrt{u}(-\\frac{1}{2}\\text{d}u) \\\\  & =4\\pi {\\int }_{12}^{16}{u}^{1/2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =4\\pi \\cdot \\frac{2}{3}{[{u}^{3/2}]}_{12}^{16} \\\\  & =\\frac{8\\pi }{3}[64-24\\sqrt{3}] \\\\  & =\\frac{64\\pi (8-3\\sqrt{3})}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Let ${S}_{2}$ denote the bottom surface. On this surface, we have $z=0$ for ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 4.$ Therefore, for everywhere on ${S}_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\nF(x,y,0)=⟨0,\\,0,\\,{0}^{2}\\sqrt{16-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}⟩=⟨0,\\,0,\\,0⟩.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{2}}0\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{2}}0\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S+{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =\\frac{64\\pi (8-3\\sqrt{3})}{3}+0 \\\\  & =\\frac{64\\pi (8-3\\sqrt{3})}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068126/state.json" "file_sha256" "1a50a43e95841a894c960cbfa2978f95d81e693b1c46f1daf30209d69ffe587e"} {"question" "q-161207" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068126/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "d6d75d30e7c8717e7da46673aa7f12d35bcdbdfe33e4bb63e23f33b6072f04ce"} {"question" "q-161207" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068126/state.json" "file_sha256" "1a50a43e95841a894c960cbfa2978f95d81e693b1c46f1daf30209d69ffe587e"} {"question" "q-161207" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "5\\pi "] ["math" "\\frac{27\\pi (4-\\sqrt{2})}{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{5\\pi +3}{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{64\\pi (8-3\\sqrt{3})}{3}"] ["math" "\\pi +3\\sqrt{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068126/state.json" "file_sha256" "1a50a43e95841a894c960cbfa2978f95d81e693b1c46f1daf30209d69ffe587e"} {"question" "q-161207" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{64\\pi (8-3\\sqrt{3})}{3}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068126/state.json" "file_sha256" "1a50a43e95841a894c960cbfa2978f95d81e693b1c46f1daf30209d69ffe587e"} {"question" "q-162550" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068126/assets/fa7f40460a8834c73c61575aef821157e1aff7dace359752fd98593271812c10.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨0,y({x}^{2}+{z}^{2}+4),\\,0⟩$ and the surface $S$ defined by the bounding region of a right circular cylinder of height $2$ whose base is a circle of radius $1$ centered at the origin, as shown above. Calculate the flux of $F$ through $S.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068126/state.json" "file_sha256" "1a50a43e95841a894c960cbfa2978f95d81e693b1c46f1daf30209d69ffe587e"} {"question" "q-162550" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $n,$ and $F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nNotice that only the $y$-component of $F=⟨0,y({x}^{2}+{z}^{2}+4),\\,0⟩$ is non-zero. Since the lateral side of $S$ is parallel to the $y$-axis, the flux of $F$ through $S$ will be non-zero only at the left and right sides. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068126/assets/1f00f0e9fa8148ca3cf161322c926ea9fc16a4467991063cbdcb4f293254674d.png)\n\nLet's consider the flux of $F$ through these two sides.\n\n- Let ${S}_{1}$ denote the right surface. On this surface, we have $y=2$ for ${x}^{2}+{z}^{2}\\le 1.$ The outward-pointing unit normal vector to ${S}_{1}$ is\n\n$$\n{n}_{1}=⟨0,\\,1,\\,0⟩.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $y=2$ on ${S}_{1},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot {n}_{1} & =⟨0,\\,y({x}^{2}+{z}^{2}+4),\\,0⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,1,\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,2({x}^{2}+{z}^{2}+4),\\,0⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,1,\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =2({x}^{2}+{z}^{2}+4).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the flux of $F$ across ${S}_{1}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}{S}_{1} & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}2({x}^{2}+{z}^{2}+4)\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =2{∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+{z}^{2}+4)\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying a change of variables $(x,z)\\to (r,\\theta ),\\text{d}A=r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+{z}^{2}+4)\\,\\text{d}A & =2{∬}_{D}({r}^{2}+4)\\cdot r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{1}r({r}^{2}+4)\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{1}({r}^{3}+4r)\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}\\theta {\\int }_{0}^{1}({r}^{3}+4r)\\,\\text{d}r \\\\  & =2\\cdot 2\\pi \\cdot {[\\frac{1}{4}{r}^{4}+2{r}^{2}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =4\\pi (\\frac{1}{4}+2) \\\\  & =9\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Let ${S}_{2}$ denote the left surface. On this surface, we have $y=0$ for ${x}^{2}+{z}^{2}\\le 1.$ Therefore, for everywhere on ${S}_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\nF(x,0,z)=⟨0,\\,0({x}^{2}+{z}^{2}+4),\\,0⟩=⟨0,\\,0,\\,0⟩.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{2}}0\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{2}}0\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S+{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =9\\pi +0 \\\\  & =9\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068126/state.json" "file_sha256" "1a50a43e95841a894c960cbfa2978f95d81e693b1c46f1daf30209d69ffe587e"} {"question" "q-162550" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068126/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "d6d75d30e7c8717e7da46673aa7f12d35bcdbdfe33e4bb63e23f33b6072f04ce"} {"question" "q-162550" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068126/state.json" "file_sha256" "1a50a43e95841a894c960cbfa2978f95d81e693b1c46f1daf30209d69ffe587e"} {"question" "q-162550" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "4\\pi "] ["math" "6\\pi "] ["math" "9\\pi "] ["math" "\\frac{3\\pi }{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{8\\pi }{3}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068126/state.json" "file_sha256" "1a50a43e95841a894c960cbfa2978f95d81e693b1c46f1daf30209d69ffe587e"} {"question" "q-162550" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "9\\pi "] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068126/state.json" "file_sha256" "1a50a43e95841a894c960cbfa2978f95d81e693b1c46f1daf30209d69ffe587e"} {"question" "q-161171" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068126/assets/d0f6f5ed91981e15d2fb80e2630cec6d20f566feb627899f529e07bab12b73fb.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨0,\\,0,\\,z({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})⟩$ and the surface $S$ defined by the bounding region of a right circular cylinder of height $2$ whose base is a circle of radius $1$ centered at the origin, as shown above. Calculate the flux of $F$ through $S.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068126/state.json" "file_sha256" "1a50a43e95841a894c960cbfa2978f95d81e693b1c46f1daf30209d69ffe587e"} {"question" "q-161171" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $n,$ and $F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nNotice that only the $z$-component of $F=⟨0,\\,0,\\,z({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})⟩$ is non-zero. Since the lateral side of $S$ is parallel to the $z$-axis, the flux of $F$ through $S$ will be non-zero only at the top and bottom sides. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068126/assets/c7b22b03b9952bfe068e9252f7336568aa8d761c32a1bc31c444ebb97ee925ba.png)\n\n \nLet's consider the flux of $F$ through these two sides.\n\n- Let ${S}_{1}$ denote the top surface. On this surface, we have $z=2$ for ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 1.$ The outward-pointing unit normal vector to ${S}_{1}$ is\n\n$$\n{n}_{1}=⟨0,\\,0,\\,1⟩.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $z=2$ on ${S}_{1},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot {n}_{1} & =⟨0,\\,0,\\,z({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,0,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,0,\\,2({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,0,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =2({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the flux of $F$ across ${S}_{1}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}2({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =2{∬}_{D}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying a change of variables $(x,y)\\to (r,\\theta ),\\text{d}A=r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{∬}_{D}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A & =2{∬}_{D}{r}^{2}\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{1}{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}\\theta {\\int }_{0}^{1}{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r \\\\  & =2\\cdot 2\\pi \\cdot {[\\frac{1}{4}{r}^{4}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =4\\pi \\cdot \\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Let ${S}_{2}$ denote the bottom surface. On this surface, we have $z=0$ for $0\\le {x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 1.$ Therefore, for everywhere on ${S}_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\nF(x,y,0)=⟨0,\\,0,\\,0\\cdot ({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})⟩=⟨0,\\,0,\\,0⟩.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{2}}0\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{2}}0\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S+{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =\\pi +0 \\\\  & =\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068126/state.json" "file_sha256" "1a50a43e95841a894c960cbfa2978f95d81e693b1c46f1daf30209d69ffe587e"} {"question" "q-161171" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068126/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "d6d75d30e7c8717e7da46673aa7f12d35bcdbdfe33e4bb63e23f33b6072f04ce"} {"question" "q-161171" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068126/state.json" "file_sha256" "1a50a43e95841a894c960cbfa2978f95d81e693b1c46f1daf30209d69ffe587e"} {"question" "q-161171" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-\\pi "] ["math" "0"] ["math" "1"] ["math" "2\\pi "] ["math" "\\pi "]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068126/state.json" "file_sha256" "1a50a43e95841a894c960cbfa2978f95d81e693b1c46f1daf30209d69ffe587e"} {"question" "q-161171" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\pi "] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068126/state.json" "file_sha256" "1a50a43e95841a894c960cbfa2978f95d81e693b1c46f1daf30209d69ffe587e"} {"question" "q-161215" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068126/assets/534c80a8318ebfe21ba3f21f04ae145f999d6d83c34a402294c789bb2090e014.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨0,\\,0,\\,z{e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}⟩$ and the surface $S$ defined by the bounding region of a right circular cylinder of height $3$ whose base is a circle of radius $1$ centered at the origin, as shown above. Calculate the flux of $F$ through $S.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068126/state.json" "file_sha256" "1a50a43e95841a894c960cbfa2978f95d81e693b1c46f1daf30209d69ffe587e"} {"question" "q-161215" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $n,$ and $F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nNotice that only the $z$-component of $F=⟨0,\\,0,\\,z{e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}⟩$ is non-zero. Since the lateral side of $S$ is parallel to the $z$-axis, the flux of $F$ through $S$ will be non-zero only at the top and bottom sides. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068126/assets/7f44bc35687020594b0d26a2e25b9dbd2b8d75c964a90184626afad6afc1b1e8.png)\n\n \nLet's consider the flux of $F$ through these two sides.\n\n- Let ${S}_{1}$ denote the top surface. On this surface, we have $z=3$ for ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 1.$ The outward-pointing unit normal vector to ${S}_{1}$ is\n\n$$\n{n}_{1}=⟨0,\\,0,\\,1⟩.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $z=3$ on ${S}_{1},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot {n}_{1} & =⟨0,\\,0,\\,z{e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,0,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,0,\\,3{e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,0,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =3{e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the flux of $F$ across ${S}_{1}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}3{e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =3{∬}_{D}{e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying a change of variables $(x,y)\\to (r,\\theta ),\\text{d}A=r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{∬}_{D}{e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A & =3{∬}_{D}{e}^{{r}^{2}}\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{1}r{e}^{{r}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}\\theta {\\int }_{0}^{1}r{e}^{{r}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}r \\\\  & =3\\cdot 2\\pi \\cdot {[\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{{r}^{2}}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =3\\pi (e-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Let ${S}_{2}$ denote the bottom surface. On this surface, we have $z=0$ for ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 1.$ Therefore, for everywhere on ${S}_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\nF(x,y,0)=⟨0,\\,0,\\,0{e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}⟩=⟨0,\\,0,\\,0⟩.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{2}}0\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{2}}0\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S+{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =3\\pi (e-1)+0 \\\\  & =3\\pi (e-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068126/state.json" "file_sha256" "1a50a43e95841a894c960cbfa2978f95d81e693b1c46f1daf30209d69ffe587e"} {"question" "q-161215" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068126/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "d6d75d30e7c8717e7da46673aa7f12d35bcdbdfe33e4bb63e23f33b6072f04ce"} {"question" "q-161215" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068126/state.json" "file_sha256" "1a50a43e95841a894c960cbfa2978f95d81e693b1c46f1daf30209d69ffe587e"} {"question" "q-161215" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2\\pi ({e}^{2}-2)"] ["math" "3\\pi (e-1)"] ["math" "3\\pi e-2"] ["math" "3\\pi {e}^{2}-1"] ["math" "\\pi e-3"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068126/state.json" "file_sha256" "1a50a43e95841a894c960cbfa2978f95d81e693b1c46f1daf30209d69ffe587e"} {"question" "q-161215" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "3\\pi (e-1)"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068126/state.json" "file_sha256" "1a50a43e95841a894c960cbfa2978f95d81e693b1c46f1daf30209d69ffe587e"} {"question" "q-161241" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068126/assets/6bd0ac15180df7a413691a1f1f15980b47e88c63e1d3af914175231c172ca97c.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨x{z}^{2},\\,0,\\,-{x}^{2}z⟩$ and the boundary $S$ of a spherical solid of radius $3$ centered at the origin. Calculate the flux of $F$ through $S.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068126/state.json" "file_sha256" "1a50a43e95841a894c960cbfa2978f95d81e693b1c46f1daf30209d69ffe587e"} {"question" "q-161241" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $n,$ and $F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nFor a sphere or radius $r$ centered at $O,$ the unit normal to the sphere is given by\n\n$$\nn=\\frac{⟨x,y,z⟩}{r}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $r=3.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\nn=\\frac{1}{3}⟨x,y,z⟩.\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot n & =⟨x{z}^{2},\\,0,\\,-{x}^{2}z⟩\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}⟨x{z}^{2},\\,0,\\,-{x}^{2}z⟩\\cdot ⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}({x}^{2}{z}^{2}+0-{x}^{2}{z}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the flux of $F$ across $S$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{S}0\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068126/state.json" "file_sha256" "1a50a43e95841a894c960cbfa2978f95d81e693b1c46f1daf30209d69ffe587e"} {"question" "q-161241" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068126/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "d6d75d30e7c8717e7da46673aa7f12d35bcdbdfe33e4bb63e23f33b6072f04ce"} {"question" "q-161241" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068126/state.json" "file_sha256" "1a50a43e95841a894c960cbfa2978f95d81e693b1c46f1daf30209d69ffe587e"} {"question" "q-161241" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "0"] ["math" "12\\pi "] ["math" "18\\pi "] ["math" "3\\pi "] ["math" "4\\pi \\sqrt{3}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068126/state.json" "file_sha256" "1a50a43e95841a894c960cbfa2978f95d81e693b1c46f1daf30209d69ffe587e"} {"question" "q-161241" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "0"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068126/state.json" "file_sha256" "1a50a43e95841a894c960cbfa2978f95d81e693b1c46f1daf30209d69ffe587e"} {"question" "q-161238" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068126/assets/5f393842a0e53fcacf439250245935fe7a7ad068973ee5f896f11916c3881e57.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨x+xz,\\,y,\\,z-{x}^{2}⟩$ and the boundary $S$ of a spherical solid of radius $\\sqrt{5}$ centered at the origin. Calculate the flux of $F$ through $S.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068126/state.json" "file_sha256" "1a50a43e95841a894c960cbfa2978f95d81e693b1c46f1daf30209d69ffe587e"} {"question" "q-161238" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $n,$ and $F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nFor a sphere or radius $r$ centered at $O,$ the unit normal to the sphere is given by\n\n$$\nn=\\frac{⟨x,y,z⟩}{r}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $r=\\sqrt{5}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\nn=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}⟨x,y,z⟩.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=5$ on $S,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot n & =⟨x+xz,\\,y,\\,z-{x}^{2}⟩\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}⟨x+xz,\\,y,\\,z-{x}^{2}⟩\\cdot ⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}(x(x+xz)+{y}^{2}+z(z-{x}^{2})) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}({x}^{2}+{x}^{2}z+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}-{x}^{2}z) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the flux of $F$ across $S$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{S}\\sqrt{5}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5}{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5}\\cdot \\text{Area}(S) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5}\\cdot 4\\pi \\cdot (\\sqrt{5})^{2} \\\\  & =20\\pi \\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068126/state.json" "file_sha256" "1a50a43e95841a894c960cbfa2978f95d81e693b1c46f1daf30209d69ffe587e"} {"question" "q-161238" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068126/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "d6d75d30e7c8717e7da46673aa7f12d35bcdbdfe33e4bb63e23f33b6072f04ce"} {"question" "q-161238" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068126/state.json" "file_sha256" "1a50a43e95841a894c960cbfa2978f95d81e693b1c46f1daf30209d69ffe587e"} {"question" "q-161238" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "15\\pi "] ["math" "20\\pi \\sqrt{5}"] ["math" "3\\pi "] ["math" "5\\pi \\sqrt{5}"] ["math" "9\\pi \\sqrt{5}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068126/state.json" "file_sha256" "1a50a43e95841a894c960cbfa2978f95d81e693b1c46f1daf30209d69ffe587e"} {"question" "q-161238" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "20\\pi \\sqrt{5}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068126/state.json" "file_sha256" "1a50a43e95841a894c960cbfa2978f95d81e693b1c46f1daf30209d69ffe587e"} {"question" "q-161232" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068126/assets/ea320b4b8a036bdf905a51041eb94ffb2464125af912ec74830d49af922f4342.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨x,\\,y+2z,\\,z-2y⟩$ and the boundary $S$ of a spherical solid of radius $\\sqrt{3}$ centered at the origin. Calculate the flux of $F$ through $S.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068126/state.json" "file_sha256" "1a50a43e95841a894c960cbfa2978f95d81e693b1c46f1daf30209d69ffe587e"} {"question" "q-161232" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $n,$ and $F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nFor a sphere or radius $r$ centered at $O,$ the unit normal to the sphere is given by\n\n$$\nn=\\frac{⟨x,y,z⟩}{r}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $r=\\sqrt{3}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\nn=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}⟨x,y,z⟩.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=3$ on $S,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot n & =⟨x,\\,y+2z,\\,z-2y⟩\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}⟨x,\\,y+2z,\\,z-2y⟩\\cdot ⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}({x}^{2}+y(y+2z)+z(z-2y)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+2yz+{z}^{2}-2yz) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the flux of $F$ across $S$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{S}\\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}\\cdot \\text{Area}(S) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}\\cdot 4\\pi \\cdot (\\sqrt{3})^{2} \\\\  & =12\\pi \\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068126/state.json" "file_sha256" "1a50a43e95841a894c960cbfa2978f95d81e693b1c46f1daf30209d69ffe587e"} {"question" "q-161232" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068126/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "d6d75d30e7c8717e7da46673aa7f12d35bcdbdfe33e4bb63e23f33b6072f04ce"} {"question" "q-161232" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068126/state.json" "file_sha256" "1a50a43e95841a894c960cbfa2978f95d81e693b1c46f1daf30209d69ffe587e"} {"question" "q-161232" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "12\\pi "] ["math" "12\\pi \\sqrt{3}"] ["math" "36\\pi "] ["math" "4\\pi \\sqrt{3}"] ["math" "8\\pi \\sqrt{3}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068126/state.json" "file_sha256" "1a50a43e95841a894c960cbfa2978f95d81e693b1c46f1daf30209d69ffe587e"} {"question" "q-161232" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "12\\pi \\sqrt{3}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068126/state.json" "file_sha256" "1a50a43e95841a894c960cbfa2978f95d81e693b1c46f1daf30209d69ffe587e"} {"question" "q-161236" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068126/assets/e6c13f4405c407123973c370a20c88b9c989bd54ade909b06414eabc22f0d45e.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨x,\\,y-{z}^{2},\\,z(1+y)⟩$ and the boundary $S$ of a spherical solid of radius $1$ centered at the origin. Calculate the flux of $F$ through $S.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068126/state.json" "file_sha256" "1a50a43e95841a894c960cbfa2978f95d81e693b1c46f1daf30209d69ffe587e"} {"question" "q-161236" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $n,$ and $F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nFor a sphere or radius $r$ centered at $O,$ the unit normal to the sphere is given by\n\n$$\nn=\\frac{⟨x,y,z⟩}{r}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $r=1.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\nn=⟨x,y,z⟩.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=1$ on $S,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot n & =⟨x,\\,y-{z}^{2},\\,z(1+y)⟩\\cdot ⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+y(y-{z}^{2})+{z}^{2}(1+y) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-y{z}^{2}+{z}^{2}+y{z}^{2} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the flux of $F$ across $S$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{S}1\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =\\text{Area}(S) \\\\  & =4\\pi \\cdot {1}^{2} \\\\  & =4\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068126/state.json" "file_sha256" "1a50a43e95841a894c960cbfa2978f95d81e693b1c46f1daf30209d69ffe587e"} {"question" "q-161236" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068126/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "d6d75d30e7c8717e7da46673aa7f12d35bcdbdfe33e4bb63e23f33b6072f04ce"} {"question" "q-161236" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068126/state.json" "file_sha256" "1a50a43e95841a894c960cbfa2978f95d81e693b1c46f1daf30209d69ffe587e"} {"question" "q-161236" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "12\\pi "] ["math" "2\\pi "] ["math" "4\\pi "] ["math" "6\\pi "] ["math" "8\\pi "]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068126/state.json" "file_sha256" "1a50a43e95841a894c960cbfa2978f95d81e693b1c46f1daf30209d69ffe587e"} {"question" "q-161236" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "4\\pi "] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068126/state.json" "file_sha256" "1a50a43e95841a894c960cbfa2978f95d81e693b1c46f1daf30209d69ffe587e"} {"question" "q-161233" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068126/assets/6bd0ac15180df7a413691a1f1f15980b47e88c63e1d3af914175231c172ca97c.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨x-y,\\,y+x,\\,z⟩$ and the boundary $S$ of a spherical solid of radius $3$ centered at the origin. Calculate the flux of $F$ through $S.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068126/state.json" "file_sha256" "1a50a43e95841a894c960cbfa2978f95d81e693b1c46f1daf30209d69ffe587e"} {"question" "q-161233" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $n,$ and $F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nFor a sphere or radius $r$ centered at $O,$ the unit normal to the sphere is given by\n\n$$\nn=\\frac{⟨x,y,z⟩}{r}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $r=3.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\nn=\\frac{1}{3}⟨x,y,z⟩.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=9$ on $S,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot n & =⟨x-y,\\,y+x,\\,z⟩\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}⟨x-y,\\,y+x,\\,z⟩\\cdot ⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(x(x-y)+y(y+x)+{z}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}({x}^{2}-xy+{y}^{2}+xy+{z}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot 9 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the flux of $F$ across $S$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{S}3\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =3{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =3\\cdot \\text{Area}(S) \\\\  & =3\\cdot 4\\pi \\cdot {3}^{2} \\\\  & =108\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068126/state.json" "file_sha256" "1a50a43e95841a894c960cbfa2978f95d81e693b1c46f1daf30209d69ffe587e"} {"question" "q-161233" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068126/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "d6d75d30e7c8717e7da46673aa7f12d35bcdbdfe33e4bb63e23f33b6072f04ce"} {"question" "q-161233" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068126/state.json" "file_sha256" "1a50a43e95841a894c960cbfa2978f95d81e693b1c46f1daf30209d69ffe587e"} {"question" "q-161233" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "108\\pi "] ["math" "12\\pi \\sqrt{3}"] ["math" "36\\pi "] ["math" "36\\pi \\sqrt{3}"] ["math" "92\\pi "]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068126/state.json" "file_sha256" "1a50a43e95841a894c960cbfa2978f95d81e693b1c46f1daf30209d69ffe587e"} {"question" "q-161233" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "108\\pi "] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068126/state.json" "file_sha256" "1a50a43e95841a894c960cbfa2978f95d81e693b1c46f1daf30209d69ffe587e"} {"question" "e-12385" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068126/assets/bd504e1d8d85a2f2bbb4b75bd6825120a6aef15345c704714931b6270f84796f.png)\n\n \n\nConsider the vector field $\\mathbf F(x,y,z) = \\langle 0, \\: 0, \\: 1 + xyz \\rangle$ and the surface $S$ defined by the bounding region of the rectangular solid shown above in the first octant. Calculate the flux of $\\mathbf F$ through $S.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068126/example-12385.json" "file_sha256" "e979a61af70ef18ff8be7e7d59d63ca02b133c5a3eec085a403af0cf9001e4d0"} {"question" "e-12385" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $\\mathbf n,$ and $\\mathbf F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $\\mathbf n$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{S}\n=\n\\iint\\limits_S \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\,\\textrm{d}S.\n$$\n\nNotice that only the $z$-component of $\\mathbf F =  \\langle 0,\\: 0,\\: 1+xyz\\rangle$ is non-zero. Since the lateral sides of $S$ are parallel to the $z$-axis, the flux of $\\mathbf F$ through $S$ will be non-zero only at the top and bottom sides. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068126/assets/977d93ad65c9f3690def4242f98f4de5e5c89faf02fe47d06f5ed251d8a4a292.png)\n\n \n\nLet's consider the flux of $\\mathbf F$ through these two sides.\n\n- Let $S_1$ denote the top surface. On this surface, we have $z = 1$ for $0 \\leq x \\leq 1$ and $0 \\leq y \\leq 1.$ The outward-pointing unit normal vector to $S_1$ is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf n_1 = \\langle 0,\\, 0,\\, 1\\rangle.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $z = 1$ on $S_1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot {n}_{1} & =⟨0,\\,0,\\,1+xyz⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,0,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,0,\\,1+xy⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,0,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =1+xy.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $S_1$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}1+xy\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}1+xy\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}1+xy\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{[x+\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}y]}_{x=0}^{x=1}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}1+\\frac{1}{2}y\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={[y+\\frac{1}{4}{y}^{2}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Let $S_2$ denote the bottom surface. On this surface, we have $z = 0$ for $0 \\leq x \\leq 1$ and $0 \\leq y \\leq 1.$ The outward-pointing unit normal vector to $S_2$ is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf n_2 = \\langle 0,\\, 0,\\, -1\\rangle.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $z = 0$ on $S_2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot {n}_{2} & =⟨0,\\,0,\\,1+xyz⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,0,\\,-1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,0,\\,1⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,0,\\,-1⟩ \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $S_2$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}{S}_{2} & ={∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{2}}-1\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}-1\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-{∬}_{D}\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-\\text{Area}(D) \\\\  & =-(1-0)(1-0) \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S+{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{4}+(-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068126/example-12385.json" "file_sha256" "e979a61af70ef18ff8be7e7d59d63ca02b133c5a3eec085a403af0cf9001e4d0"} {"question" "e-12453" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068126/assets/925b2f032f751e4482632c449fff35f544929f3355655c20d6701e640a7b20a3.png)\n\n \n\nConsider the vector field $\\mathbf F(x,y,z) = \\langle 0,\\: 0,\\: z\\sqrt{x^2 + y^2} \\rangle$ and the surface $S$ defined by the bounding region of a right circular cylinder of height $4$ whose base is a circle of radius $1$ centered at the origin, as shown above. Calculate the flux of $\\mathbf F$ through $S.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068126/example-12453.json" "file_sha256" "1bce1ce62a5f096458a1aad987ec61b24bbeda22dfb90d1b59ccb5182750fef7"} {"question" "e-12453" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $\\mathbf n,$ and $\\mathbf F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $\\mathbf n$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{S} =\n\\iint\\limits_S \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\,\\textrm{d}S.\n$$\n\nNotice that only the $z$-component of $\\mathbf F =  \\langle 0,\\: 0,\\:  z\\sqrt{x^2 + y^2} \\rangle$ is non-zero. Since the lateral side of $S$ is parallel to the $z$-axis, the flux of $\\mathbf F$ through $S$ will be non-zero only at the top and bottom sides. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068126/assets/f5a443b0bb234662c8f4a2bc156b458ac09a8db3ce3cc67933f7465a19db2ef9.png)\n\n \nLet's consider the flux of $\\mathbf F$ through these two sides.\n\n- Let $S_1$ denote the top surface. On this surface, we have $z = 4$ for $x^2 + y^2 \\leq 1.$ The outward-pointing unit normal vector to $S_1$ is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf n_1 = \\langle 0,\\, 0,\\, 1\\rangle.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $z = 4$ on $S_1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot {n}_{1} & =⟨0,\\,0,\\,z\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,0,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,0,\\,4\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,0,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $S_1$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}4\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =4{∬}_{D}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying a change of variables $(x,y) \\to (r,\\theta), \\textrm d A = r\\,\\textrm d r\\,\\textrm d \\theta,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{∬}_{D}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A & =4{∬}_{D}r\\cdot r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{1}{r}^{2}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}\\theta {\\int }_{0}^{1}{r}^{2}\\,\\text{d}r \\\\  & =4\\cdot 2\\pi \\cdot {[\\frac{1}{3}{r}^{3}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =8\\pi (\\frac{1}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{8\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Let $S_2$ denote the bottom surface. On this surface, we have $z = 0$ for $x^2 + y^2 \\leq 1.$ Therefore, for every point on $S_2,$ we have\n\n$$\nF(x,y,0) = \\langle 0,\\: 0,\\: 0 \\sqrt{x^2 + y^2} \\rangle =  \\langle 0,\\: 0,\\: 0\\rangle.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{2}}0\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{2}}0\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S+{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =\\frac{8\\pi }{3}+0 \\\\  & =\\frac{8\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068126/example-12453.json" "file_sha256" "1bce1ce62a5f096458a1aad987ec61b24bbeda22dfb90d1b59ccb5182750fef7"} {"question" "e-12386" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068126/assets/e6c13f4405c407123973c370a20c88b9c989bd54ade909b06414eabc22f0d45e.png)\n\n \n\nConsider the vector field $\\mathbf F(x,y,z) = \\langle 2x,\\: 2y+xz,\\: 2z-xy\\rangle$ and the boundary $S$ of a spherical solid of radius $1$ centered at the origin. Calculate the flux of $\\mathbf F$ through $S.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068126/example-12386.json" "file_sha256" "15f5f7fa4e87209faa0f03c5798b4d564411b9af996b43c0b8eb36abdb53fe9c"} {"question" "e-12386" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $\\mathbf n,$ and $\\mathbf F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $\\mathbf n$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{S}\n=\n\\iint\\limits_S \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\,\\textrm{d}S.\n$$\n\nFor a sphere or radius $r$ centered at $O,$ the unit normal to the sphere is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf n = \\dfrac{\\langle x, y, z\\rangle}{r}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $r=1.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\mathbf n = \\langle x, y, z\\rangle.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $x^2+y^2+z^2=1$ on $S,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot n & =⟨2x,\\,2y+xz,\\,2z-xy⟩\\cdot ⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =2{x}^{2}+(2y+xz)\\cdot y+(2z-xy)\\cdot z \\\\  & =2{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}+xyz+2{z}^{2}-xyz \\\\  & =2({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}) \\\\  & =2\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $S$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{S}2\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =2{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\text{Area}(S) \\\\  & =2\\cdot 4\\pi \\cdot {1}^{2} \\\\  & =8\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068126/example-12386.json" "file_sha256" "15f5f7fa4e87209faa0f03c5798b4d564411b9af996b43c0b8eb36abdb53fe9c"}] "usage" {"e-12386" [] "q-161232" [] "q-162550" [] "q-161162" [] "e-12385" [] "q-161171" [] "e-12453" []} "basis" 2275 "retractions" [[17592186198528 :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/4162/Source/Images/q-161162.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F(x, y, z) = ⟨0, 0, 2x + yz⟩$ and the surface $S$ defined by the bounding region of the rectangular solid shown above in the first octant. Calculate the flux of $F$ through $S$."] [17592186198554 :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/4162/Source/Images/q-162550.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F(x, y, z) = ⟨0, y(x^{2} + z^{2} + 4), 0⟩$ and the surface $S$ defined by the bounding region of a right circular cylinder of height $2$ whose base is a circle of radius $1$ centered at the origin, as shown above. Calculate the flux of $F$ through $S$."] [17592186198531 :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/4162/Source/Images/q-161171.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F(x, y, z) = ⟨0, 0, z(x^{2} + y^{2})⟩$ and the surface $S$ defined by the bounding region of a right circular cylinder of height $2$ whose base is a circle of radius $1$ centered at the origin, as shown above. Calculate the flux of $F$ through $S$."] [17592186198533 :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/4162/Source/Images/q-161232.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F(x, y, z) = ⟨x, y + 2z, z - 2y⟩$ and the boundary $S$ of a spherical solid of radius $\\sqrt{3}$ centered at the origin. Calculate the flux of $F$ through $S$."] [17592186197960 :question/problem "Consider the vector field $\\mathbf F(x,y,z) = \\langle 0, \\: 0, \\: 1 + xyz \\rangle$ and the surface $S$ defined by the bounding region of the rectangular solid shown above in the first octant. Calculate the flux of $\\mathbf F$ through $S$.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/4162/Source/Images/cac638a0e26e72df4bdfca80611d5bba.png)"] [17592186197960 :question/worked-solution "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $\\mathbf n$, and $\\mathbf F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $\\mathbf n$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{S} = \\iint\\limits_S \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\,\\textrm{d}S\n$$\n\nNotice that only the $z$-component of\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F = \\langle 0,\\: 0,\\: 1+xyz\\rangle\n$$\n\nis non-zero. Since the lateral sides of $S$ are parallel to the $z$-axis, the flux of $\\mathbf F$ through $S$ will be non-zero only at the top and bottom sides.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/4162/Source/Images/af13f1982951f06cd09e8d9249599d81.png)\n\nLet's consider the flux of $\\mathbf F$ through these two sides.\n\n- Let $S_1$ denote the top surface. On this surface, we have $z = 1$ for $0 \\leq x \\leq 1$ and $0 \\leq y \\leq 1$. The outward-pointing unit normal vector to $S_1$ is\n$\\mathbf n_1 = \\langle 0,\\, 0,\\, 1\\rangle$.\nUsing the fact that $z = 1$ on $S_1$, we have\n$F \\cdot n_{1}|= ⟨0, 0, 1 + xyz⟩ \\cdot ⟨0, 0, 1⟩; = ⟨0, 0, 1 + xy⟩ \\cdot ⟨0, 0, 1⟩; = 1 + xy$.\nTherefore, we can calculate the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $S_1$ as follows:\n$∬_(S_{1})F \\cdot dS|= ∬_(S_{1})F \\cdot n_{1}dS; = ∬_(S_{1})1 + xydS; = ∬_(D)1 + xydA; = ∫_{0}^{1}∫_{0}^{1}1 + xydxdy; = ∫_{0}^{1}[x + \\frac{1}{2}x^{2}y]_{x = 0}^{x = 1}dy; = ∫_{0}^{1}1 + \\frac{1}{2}ydy; = [y + \\frac{1}{4}y^{2}]_{0}^{1}; = \\frac{5}{4}$\n- Let $S_2$ denote the bottom surface. On this surface, we have $z = 0$ for $0 \\leq x \\leq 1$ and $0 \\leq y \\leq 1$. The outward-pointing unit normal vector to $S_2$ is\n$\\mathbf n_2 = \\langle 0,\\, 0,\\, -1\\rangle$.\nUsing the fact that $z = 0$ on $S_2$, we have\n$F \\cdot n_{2}|= ⟨0, 0, 1 + xyz⟩ \\cdot ⟨0, 0,-1⟩; = ⟨0, 0, 1⟩ \\cdot ⟨0, 0,-1⟩; =-1$.\nTherefore, we can calculate the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $S_2$ as follows:\n$∬_(S_{2})F \\cdot dS_{2}|= ∬_(S_{2})F \\cdot n_{2}dS; = ∬_(S_{2}) - 1dS; = ∬_(D) - 1dA; = - ∬_(D)dA; =-Area(D); = - (1 - 0)(1 - 0); =-1$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∬_(S)F \\cdot dS &= ∬_(S)F \\cdot ndS \\\\\n&= ∬_(S_{1})F \\cdot ndS + ∬_(S_{2})F \\cdot ndS \\\\\n&= \\frac{5}{4} + (-1) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{4}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186197974 :question/problem "Consider the vector field $\\mathbf F(x,y,z) = \\langle 0,\\: 0,\\: z\\sqrt{x^2 + y^2} \\rangle$ and the surface $S$ defined by the bounding region of a right circular cylinder of height $4$ whose base is a circle of radius $1$ centered at the origin, as shown above. Calculate the flux of $\\mathbf F$ through $S$.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/4162/Source/Images/0b8c76a27f89a5bf55914475ba7b8324.png)"] [17592186197974 :question/worked-solution "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $\\mathbf n$, and $\\mathbf F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $\\mathbf n$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{S} = \\iint\\limits_S \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\,\\textrm{d}S\n$$\n\nNotice that only the $z$-component of\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F = \\langle 0,\\: 0,\\: z\\sqrt{x^2 + y^2} \\rangle\n$$\n\nis non-zero. Since the lateral side of $S$ is parallel to the $z$-axis, the flux of $\\mathbf F$ through $S$ will be non-zero only at the top and bottom sides.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/4162/Source/Images/f750ea73c79c9c2eddfa45ef88626057.png)\n\nLet's consider the flux of $\\mathbf F$ through these two sides.\n\n- Let $S_1$ denote the top surface. On this surface, we have $z = 4$ for $x^2 + y^2 \\leq 1$. The outward-pointing unit normal vector to $S_1$ is\n$\\mathbf n_1 = \\langle 0,\\, 0,\\, 1\\rangle$.\nUsing the fact that $z = 4$ on $S_1$, we have\n$F \\cdot n_{1}|= ⟨0, 0, z\\sqrt{x^{2} + y^{2}}⟩ \\cdot ⟨0, 0, 1⟩; = ⟨0, 0, 4\\sqrt{x^{2} + y^{2}}⟩ \\cdot ⟨0, 0, 1⟩; = 4\\sqrt{x^{2} + y^{2}}$.\nTherefore, the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $S_1$ is given by\n$∬_(S_{1})F \\cdot dS|= ∬_(S_{1})F \\cdot n_{1}dS; = ∬_(S_{1})4\\sqrt{x^{2} + y^{2}}dS; = 4∬_(D)\\sqrt{x^{2} + y^{2}}dA$.\n\nApplying a change of variables\n\n$$\n(x,y) \\to (r,\\theta), \\textrm d A = r\\,\\textrm d r\\,\\textrm d \\theta\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n4∬_(D)\\sqrt{x^{2} + y^{2}}dA &= 4∬_(D)r \\cdot rdrdθ \\\\\n&= 4∫_{0}^{2π}∫_{0}^{1}r^{2}drdθ \\\\\n&= 4∫_{0}^{2π}dθ∫_{0}^{1}r^{2}dr \\\\\n&= 4 \\cdot 2π \\cdot [\\frac{1}{3}r^{3}]_{0}^{1} \\\\\n&= 8π(\\frac{1}{3}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{8π}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Let $S_2$ denote the bottom surface. On this surface, we have $z = 0$ for $x^2 + y^2 \\leq 1$. Therefore, for every point on $S_2$, we have\n$F(x,y,0) = \\langle 0,\\: 0,\\: 0 \\sqrt{x^2 + y^2} \\rangle = \\langle 0,\\: 0,\\: 0\\rangle$.\nTherefore,\n$∬_(S_{2})F \\cdot ndS|= ∬_(S_{2})0 \\cdot ndS; = ∬_(S_{2})0dS; = 0$.\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∬_(S)F \\cdot dS &= ∬_(S)F \\cdot ndS \\\\\n&= ∬_(S_{1})F \\cdot ndS + ∬_(S_{2})F \\cdot ndS \\\\\n&= \\frac{8π}{3} + 0 \\\\\n&= \\frac{8π}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186197961 :question/problem "Consider the vector field $\\mathbf F(x,y,z) = \\langle 2x,\\: 2y+xz,\\: 2z-xy\\rangle$ and the boundary $S$ of a spherical solid of radius $1$ centered at the origin. Calculate the flux of $\\mathbf F$ through $S$.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/4162/Source/Images/4b1c5dcc75491a1b343943157f8828e0.png)"] [17592186197961 :question/worked-solution "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $\\mathbf n$, and $\\mathbf F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $\\mathbf n$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{S} = \\iint\\limits_S \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\,\\textrm{d}S\n$$\n\nFor a sphere or radius $r$ centered at $O$, the unit normal to the sphere is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf n = \\dfrac{\\langle x, y, z\\rangle}{r}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $r=1$. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\mathbf n = \\langle x, y, z\\rangle\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that\n\n$$\nx^2+y^2+z^2=1\n$$\n\non $S$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nF \\cdot n &= ⟨2x, 2y + xz, 2z - xy⟩ \\cdot ⟨x, y, z⟩ \\\\\n&= 2x^{2} + (2y + xz) \\cdot y + (2z - xy) \\cdot z \\\\\n&= 2x^{2} + 2y^{2} + xyz + 2z^{2} - xyz \\\\\n&= 2(x^{2} + y^{2} + z^{2}) \\\\\n&= 2 \\cdot 1 \\\\\n&= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $S$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∬_(S)F \\cdot dS &= ∬_(S)F \\cdot ndS \\\\\n&= ∬_(S)2dS \\\\\n&= 2∬_(S)dS \\\\\n&= 2 \\cdot Area(S) \\\\\n&= 2 \\cdot 4π \\cdot 1^{2} \\\\\n&= 8π\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"]] "immutable_answers" [17592186194070 17592186194071 17592186194072 17592186194073 17592186194074 17592186194088 17592186194089 17592186194090 17592186194091 17592186194092 17592186194100 17592186194101 17592186194102 17592186194103 17592186194104 17592186194226 17592186194227 17592186194228 17592186194229 17592186194230] "immutable_fields" [[17592186194069 :answer-field/correct] [17592186194069 :answer-field/id] [17592186194069 :answer-field/key] [17592186194069 :answer-field/type] [17592186194087 :answer-field/correct] [17592186194087 :answer-field/id] [17592186194087 :answer-field/key] [17592186194087 :answer-field/type] [17592186194099 :answer-field/correct] [17592186194099 :answer-field/id] [17592186194099 :answer-field/key] [17592186194099 :answer-field/type] [17592186194225 :answer-field/correct] [17592186194225 :answer-field/id] [17592186194225 :answer-field/key] [17592186194225 :answer-field/type]] "no_history" []}
